			<BLOCKQUOTE>
			<B>Why do I have to click the Logoff icon/link to leave the application? Isn't closing the browser enough?</B><BR><BR>
			There are 2 reasons why it is highly recommended that users click the logoff icon once they are done with the application

			<UL>
				<LI>
					Once a user logs on to the application, the system "remembers" the state
					of the user at that particular point in time. Unless told otherwise, it
					is going to assume that user is still using the application. Since the application
					only allows a user  to be logged on in one system at a time, if the user
					does not logoff and attempts to login again, the system will give a warning
					that the user is already logged on to the system. The user is then prompted
					to reset the previous state and is forced to  log on again.<BR>
					<BR>
				</LI>
				<LI>
					The other reason has to do with the environment that users
					  find themselves in while connected to the application. Even though closing
					  the browser should be enough to reset the "user state", we find that users,
					  while connecting via certain internet  service providers or behind certain
					  firewalls and/or proxies, do not always experience the appropriate behavior.
					  To avoid this  issue, we strongly recommend that users leave the application
					  via the logoff icon/link.
				</LI>
			</UL>

			</BLOCKQUOTE>


			<BLOCKQUOTE>
			<B>In the Projects scorecard, some of the priorities have a red square next
			to them. What does it mean?</B><BR><BR>

			Senior management in your company has "flagged" certain priorities as being of a critical nature. This icon indicates that all projects
			that belong to the flagged priority, are "critical projects".
			</BLOCKQUOTE>

			<BLOCKQUOTE>
			<B>By accident I deleted last week's newsletter. How can I see it?</B><BR><BR>
			Once a newsletter is published, it is also posted, within a day or
			two, in the "What's new..." section of the application.<BR>
			<BR>


			</BLOCKQUOTE>


			<BLOCKQUOTE>
			<B>When looking at the trade history of a commodity, I'm able to download the data to Microsoft Excel. How can I do the same
			with the graph?</B><BR><BR>

			You can copy any active window and paste it into any Microsoft Office application. Specifically, you can copy the graph as a static image by doing the following:

			<UL>
				<LI>Open Microsoft Excel</LI>
				<LI>From within the <CFOUTPUT>#application.CompanyName#</CFOUTPUT>, select the commodity that you would like to capture. At this time you should be looking at the graph of the selected commodity</LI>
				<LI>On your keyboard press the keys <B>[ALT]</B>+<B>[Print Screen]</B>. The easiest way to do this is to hold the <B>[ALT]</B> key and then pressing the
					<B>[Print Screen]</B>. Some keyboards may have the <B>[Print Screen]</B> key labeled as <B>[Prt Src]</B>.</LI>
				<LI>Switch to Microsoft Excel</LI>
				<LI>From the menu, select <B>Edit</B> > <B>Paste</B>. This command pastes into Excel the snapshot of the window that had the graph</LI>
				<LI>At this time you can either leave it as is, or you can crop the image to remove the borders, title, legends etc.</LI>
			</UL>



			</BLOCKQUOTE>
			<BLOCKQUOTE>
			<B>Why is it that sometimes when I click on a news headline, I get prompted for a login and password?</B><BR><BR>
			The news headlines are provided for free by a variety of content
			providers, typically, news organizations. Many, for marketing reasons,
			request that users "sign up" to their service. These services are free, but
			the sign up process enables them to capture the users' profiles. Typically,
			once a user has signed up with a particular content provider, he/she will
			not be prompted to login again.
			</BLOCKQUOTE>



